Subject: [PATCH 15/24] fix(workspace): pin the CSP host and prove the policy
 is enforced
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three corrections to the policy added in cd92591.

connect-src named `https://*.supabase.co`, which let a hostile bundle
POST the access token to any Supabase project — the exact destination
class the doc claimed was closed, and wider than pwsApprovedOrigin()'s
own refusal of wildcard hosts. It now pins the deployment host, and
harness.js swaps in the test project the same way it already swaps
SB_URL. The wildcard-matching test keeps its coverage by swapping the
policy in and out itself, so it tests the parser without endorsing a
wildcard in what ships.

The meta tag sat after the preconnect and Google Fonts links, so those
were requested before the parser had the policy. It is now the first
element after <meta charset>. Charset stays first because it must fall
within the first 1024 bytes and the Thai comment above the policy had
pushed it to byte 1422, where Chrome guesses the encoding instead.

Every test runs over file://, which proves the parser reads the policy
but not that a browser enforces it. Served a copy over local HTTP and
probed it: fetch to a non-allowlisted host is refused by CSP (the
browser says so, rather than failing as an indistinguishable network
error) while the fonts stylesheet and the cdnjs script still load.
Evidence recorded in the doc.

Tests: node harness.js 56/56 (1 new); LC_ALL=C ./tests/run-sql-tests.sh PASS.
Data structures: unchanged. Build stamp: 2026-09-05.5.
Critical Change — auth boundary; needs CODEOWNER @phossatron review.

